>> title: New England Boiled Dinner, Slow Cooker
>> category: Stews & Braises
>> tags: beef, corned beef, cabbage, one-pot, dinner, slow cooker, crock pot
>> counters: The Slow Cooker
>> dish: new-england-boiled-dinner
>> kit: Slow Cooker
>> aka: boiled dinner, corned beef and cabbage, Yankee boiled dinner, corned beef dinner, slow cooker boiled dinner, crockpot corned beef and cabbage
>> servings: 6
>> time: 10 hr 15 min
>> slack: narrow — the staggering is the recipe: cabbage that goes in with the beef at hour zero is grey rags by hour ten and the roots are paste, and neither can be taken back out of the pot they flavoured
>> step.2: slow cook on low, 8 hr
>> step.3: roots in, 1 hr
>> step.4: cabbage in, 1 hr
>> step.5: rest 15 min, carve across the grain

This is the one file on the shelf that is not leave-it-and-go, and the price of pretending otherwise is a grey dinner. You are home for the last two hours, adding things in order — which is exactly why the dish belongs here rather than in a pressure cooker: staggering the vegetables is the whole method, and a locked lid cannot stage anything. Nothing is browned, and nothing should be: a cured brisket is simmered, never seared. The machine's real gift is that it holds a shiver it is incapable of exceeding, and a rolling boil is what squeezes brisket into string.

Slow cook @corned beef brisket{4%lb}(1.8 kg; rinsed, halved crosswise if it will not lie flat) in a #slow cooker{} on low for ~slow cook{8%hr} with @water{3%qt}(2.8 L; to cover), @bay leaves{2}, @black peppercorns{1%tsp}, @yellow onion{1%large}(halved).

Add @Yukon Gold potatoes{2%lb}(900 g; halved), @carrots{6%medium}(cut in 2-in lengths), @turnip{1%medium}(peeled and wedged) to @&(~1)pot{}, pushed down into the liquid around the beef, for ~slow cook{60%min}.

Add @green cabbage{1%small}(cut in 6 wedges through the core) to @&(~1)pot{} for ~slow cook{60%min}, until a knife goes through the stem and no longer.

Lift @&(~1)boiled dinner{} out with @flat-leaf parsley{1/4%cup}(chopped), @grainy mustard{2%Tbs}, and ~rest{15%min} before carving the brisket across the grain. Ladle a little of the pot liquid over the sliced meat and keep the rest for soup.
